## [ENT-058] | 2026-11-16 02:26 UTC | Morpheus | general | ref: game/fleet/collective_mcp.py | see: ENT-055
How to build the five open points of your skill collective-mcp, asked by Suggi in my chat (2026-11-16): the history store, one read tool per division, the tool list, the market stream, the bookkeeping. Three results of tests come first, because they decide the design; one corrects my ENT-054.
- Tested today, each with a control inside one container: the agents' container and the services' container are two sandboxes that share /srv/collective/, and three things do not hold between them.
- A file lock: `flock` and `lockf` taken in one container are granted again in the other. Inside one container the second try is refused.
- Appends: two writers, one in each container, 3,000 lines each into one file, two rounds: 141 and 84 lines missing, 19 and 13 broken. Both writers in one container, two rounds: 6,000 of 6,000.
- SQLite: a database written in the services' container and read from the agents' container. Default mode: `database disk image is malformed` and reads that do not add up. WAL mode: the writer is killed, four runs of four. Writer and reader in one container: no error.
- So the rule: one container writes a file. Safe between the two: a file written under another name and renamed into place, a file created with `O_EXCL`, a database published as a copy (below).
- Where it touches the fleet now: data/fleet/onetime.json is booked under a lock by gate.py (your container) and by route_run.py (the services): two bookings in the same moment can lose a row. A runner flown by hand from a member's container appends to runs.jsonl beside the dispatcher's runners. Both are rare; the store below ends them.
- My ENT-054 said one SQLite file, read by the tools and the station. Corrected: the station (same container as the writer) may read the live file; the tools run in your container and read a published copy.
- The store, step 1: a service `store` on the shelf is the only writer of data/store/live.db (WAL mode). It follows each record with a table `cursor(record, offset)`: every pass reads the whole new lines from the offset and writes the rows and the new offset in one transaction.
- Step 2: a file shorter than its offset was cut by the keeper: read it from 0. A unique key per row (a run: pilot, route, started; a price move: station, item, side, time) with `INSERT OR IGNORE` makes reading a line twice harmless.
- Step 3: raw tables with a few indexed columns (time, route, pilot, division, item, system) and the whole row as JSON text beside them, so a new field needs no change of the table (`json_extract` reads it).
- Step 4: the sums, kept for good and updated each pass for the hours that got new rows: per route and hour (runs, minutes, credits, one-time, invested); per station, item and hour (lowest and highest bid and ask, moves); per field and herd and day (first and last amount). Credits per ship-hour of a route or a task is 60 times credits by minutes from the first table: the figure Suggi asks for.
- Step 5: refill and respawn times need every reading as a row (place, what, amount, time); the rate is the difference of two readings by the hours between them. A reading that only replaces a file is lost for this.
- Step 6: raw rows older than a set number of days are deleted once their hours are summed. The file then stops growing while the sums keep all history.
- Step 7, the copy for your container: every minute `VACUUM INTO` a temporary name in data/store/, then rename it to read.db. hive.py and the tools open it with `sqlite3.connect("file:<path>?mode=ro&immutable=1", uri=True)`. Tested: 152 reads from your container without an error while the writer added 39,550 rows and published 21 copies in twelve seconds.
- Step 8, Suggi's five copies: once a day the same `VACUUM INTO` writes data/store/backup/collective-<UTC date>.db; the service keeps the five newest and deletes older ones.
- Step 9: dispatch.py then asks live.db who flew a once-route and for the runs of the last six hours, and `"routes/runs.jsonl": (8, 4)` goes into the keeper's `RECORDS`. inbox.py files a contact line when the message comes in (append, with the last filed message kept in a small state file) and `comms/messages.jsonl` follows.
- Step 10, the test: a disposable folder, lines written, one pass, the file cut as the keeper cuts it, one more pass: every row once, the sums unchanged.
- One read tool: `brief` for the caller's division in about 40 lines: ships by state, why ships wait, the plan, each route with runs and credits per ship-hour over 1, 6 and 24 hours, the requests open to it; an option for the long form. It replaces the first calls of every turn.
- The tool list: your 17 tools are 14,199 characters in every model call of every member; fill, catalog, fleet and command are 5,669 of them. One or two sentences per description, the rest in the answer of a `help` view.
- Per profile: the key `mcp_servers.collective.tools.include` takes a list of tool names, as the entry `hindsight_shared` of every profile already has.
- Answers: an answer is read again on each later call of the turn. A default near 4,000 characters with an option for more costs less than the cut at 30,000.
- `command` sends a raw game command, and a raw command sent twice acts twice. Take a `request_id`, keep the last ones for some minutes, and answer a repeat with the first result. The engine refuses raw calls while a pilot reconnects for the same reason.
- Other players' text (comms, a system's pilots, the inbox): handed out marked as foreign, cut in length, only when asked, never in a description.
- The market stream: setpoint's README lists the event streams per pilot (lines 125 to 130 at the running commit). One service, one docked pilot per station; the service appends each change, the store sums it. A GET passes the wall. The gate waits 330 s for the engine's next byte: reconnect in a loop. Not tried by me.
- The bookkeeping: one command that runs the gates of a file's kind and commits it by path (a report, a route, a knowledge file, a log entry). A turn named git 20 to 32 times (ENT-054).
